  "finalAnswerPreview": "## Verdict\\n\\nThe integration should expect **`InvalidCursorError`** from `@ordarium/core`, i.e. an `OrdariumError` with\\n\\n- `name === \\\"InvalidCursorError\\\"`\\n- `code === \\\"INVALID_CURSOR\\\"`\\n- message: `The change cursor is not a valid durable position; refusing to guess a resume point`\\n\\n## Where that failure comes from\\n\\nThe \\\"observation\\\" surface here is Ordarium's state change feed (`StateStore.changes(filter, cursor)` → `StateChangePage`, ORD-BOOT-0), whose `cursor` is the always-present, opaque, durable resume position a consumer is meant to store and send back. Two distinct rejections both converge on the same error, by design:\\n\\n1. **Undecodable cursor** — `decodeStateChangeCursor` in `node_modules/@ordarium/core/dist/src/ledger.js` throws `InvalidCursorError` for anything that isn't base64url of `{\\\"c\\\":\\\"<digits>\\\"}` (bad JSON, non-object, non-numeric `c`, non-safe-integer, negative).\\n2. **Decodable but semantically impossible cursor** — in `node_modules/@ordarium/ledger-sqlite/dist/src/index.js` (`changes`, ~L386–392), a position greater than the ledger's global state-change high-water mark is rejected with the same `InvalidCursorError`. That is the \\\"restored/re",
